SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP (progress, history), IEP (history), PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE, CrossRef verification
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain addresses the philosophical coherence and evidential standing of Kant's
claim that history can exhibit moral progress. The central debates turn on three
axes: (1) the epistemic status of "signs of history" (Geschichtszeichen), most
prominently the enthusiasm provoked by the French Revolution in Kant's "Renewed
Attempt" (Streit der Fakultaeten, 1798), which Kant treats as a symptom of a moral
predisposition in the human species; (2) the internal consistency of a concept of
collective moral progress within a Kantian framework that grounds morality in the
noumenal will of individuals; and (3) contemporary critiques and defenses of
Enlightenment progressivism.

The Adorno/Horkheimer critique in the Dialectic of Enlightenment (1944/47)
represents the most systematic philosophical assault on Enlightenment reason as
progressive: they argue that the domination of nature structurally entails domination
of human beings, making the Enlightenment's own self-destruction necessary rather
than accidental. More recent postcolonial critiques (Allen 2016, Marwah 2022) press
a different challenge: Kantian progress narratives are not merely internally
inconsistent but ideologically complicit with European racial hierarchies. The
teleological framework that treats European modernity as the benchmark of progress
implicates Kant's own racial theory, which constrains which peoples can participate
in moral progress. Contemporary Kantians respond either by arguing that the
progressive core can be severed from the racial ideology (Kleingeld 2001) or that
the concept of progress is best understood as a regulative and practical postulate
rather than a descriptive-historical claim (Ypi 2010, Williams 2024).

Juergen Stolzenberg's 2025 paper on enthusiasm treats the Lyotard-Kant exchange
directly: Lyotard famously argued that after Auschwitz Kant's sign of historical
progress becomes morally repugnant, but Stolzenberg argues this misunderstands the
a priori status of the ideas of freedom and justice. Kate Moran's book situates the
progress problematic within the ethical community and the highest good, while
Allen's postcolonial critique engages Frankfurt School resources to argue that the
normative foundations of critical theory must themselves be decolonized.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain is the primary locus for evaluating whether Kant's philosophy of history
constitutes a viable normative resource for contemporary political theory or whether
it is irredeemably compromised by its internal structure (the moral law's formalism
vs. empirical history) and its historical entanglement with European supremacism.
The question of whether the French Revolution functions as a genuine epistemic sign
of species-level progress — versus a rhetorical construction — is central to
assessing Kant's philosophy of history in its most politically consequential form.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Blumenberg's Legitimacy of the Modern Age (1966) and Löwith's Meaning in History
(1949) — two foundational works in the secularization-of-eschatology thesis — are
not well-represented in academic databases for philosophy journal searches; the
primary texts are not indexed by S2/OpenAlex for abstract retrieval. Secondary
literature on Löwith-Blumenberg debate was found but is largely in non-English
venues. Guyer's recent book Reason and Experience in Mendelssohn and Kant (2020)
is relevant but concerns Mendelssohn-Kant debate on individual vs. collective
progress; it is covered in domain-1 via Merritt's treatment. Steven Pinker's
Enlightenment Now (2018) is the obvious contemporary defense of progress but is
not a philosophical work; academic responses to Pinker appear but are not
philosophically focused on Kant specifically.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The domain divides clearly into: (a) internal Kantian debates about consistency and
epistemic status of progress claims; (b) Frankfurt School and postcolonial critiques;
(c) contemporary defenses that recast progress as practical postulate. Synthesis
should highlight the tension between the regulative/practical reading (which
dissolves the epistemic problem) and the postcolonial reading (which shows that
even a regulative progress idea carries ideological freight).

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Regulative/practical progressivism: 4 papers — Ypi (2010, 2024), Kleingeld (2001), Williams (2024)
- Frankfurt School critique: 4 papers — Horkheimer/Adorno, Cook, Iago, James (2018)
- Postcolonial/racial critique: 2 papers — Allen (2016), Marwah (2022)
- Community-centered Kantian progress: 2 papers — Moran (2012), Golob (2021)
- Enthusiasm and signs of progress: 2 papers — Stolzenberg (2025), Golob (2021)
====================================================================
}

@book{horkheimerAdorno2002dialectic,
  author = {Horkheimer, Max and Adorno, Theodor W.},
  title = {Dialectic of Enlightenment},
  publisher = {Stanford University Press},
  year = {2002},
  doi = {10.1515/9780804788090},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Horkheimer and Adorno argue that Enlightenment rationality, far from
  being a vehicle of emancipation, harbors a structural tendency toward self-destruction:
  the domination of external nature through instrumental reason recoils into the
  domination of human beings, producing the administered society and fascism not as
  aberrations but as consequences of Enlightenment's own logic. Myth is always already
  enlightenment (in its attempt to master fear), and enlightenment reverts to mythology
  (in its reification of facts as fate).

  RELEVANCE: The book is the most consequential philosophical attack on Kantian
  progressivism: it targets the assumption that reason, applied to history, moves toward
  freedom, and instead shows how reason's formal structure generates unfreedom. For
  Kant's philosophy of history, the Dialectic of Enlightenment poses the challenge that
  the faculty of reason Kant treats as the driver of historical progress is the same
  faculty whose systematization produces Auschwitz. Kant appears explicitly in the
  "Juliette" excursus, where the authors read Sade as Kantian morality's dark
  underside — formalism without sensuous content becomes domination.

  POSITION: Radical immanent critique of Enlightenment reason; contra Kantian progressivism.
  },
  keywords = {Frankfurt-School, Enlightenment-critique, progress, High},
  abstract = {Dialectic of Enlightenment is, quite justifiably, one of the most celebrated and often cited works of modern social philosophy. It has been identified as the keystone of the 'Frankfurt School', of which Theodor Adorno and Max Horkheimer were the leading members, and does not cease to impress in its wide-ranging ambition and panache. Adorno and Horkheimer addressees themselves to a question which went to the very heart of the modern age, namely 'why mankind, instead of entering into a truly human condition, is sinking into a new kind of barbarism'. Modernity, far from redeeming the promises and hopes of the Enlightenment, had resulted in a stultification of mankind and an administered society, characterised by simulation and candy-floss entertainment. To seek an answer to the question of how such a condition could arise, Adorno and Horkheier subjected the whole history of Western catagories of reason and nature, from Homer to Nietzsche, to a searching philosophical and psychological critique. Drawing on psychoanalytical insights, their own work on the 'culture industry', deep knowledge of the key Enlightenment and anti-Enlightenment thinkers, as well as fascinating considerations on the relationship between reason and myth - the rational and the irrational - the authors exposed the domination and violence towards both nature and humanity that underpin the Enlightenment project.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{ypi2010natura,
  author = {Ypi, Lea},
  title = {Natura Daedala Rerum? On the Justification of Historical Progress in Kant's Guarantee of Perpetual Peace},
  journal = {Kantian Review},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {14},
  number = {2},
  pages = {118--148},
  doi = {10.1017/s1369415400001497},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ypi argues that Kant's philosophy of history cannot be grounded in
  teleological claims about nature's guarantee but instead requires a practical
  constructivist justification: history's progressive direction is not discovered but
  is a normative postulate that agents committed to republican ideals must presuppose
  in order to act coherently. The article reconstructs the "Guarantee of Perpetual
  Peace" section of Perpetual Peace to show that its appeal to nature is best read as
  a device for practical motivation rather than theoretical prediction.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most influential regulative/practical reading of Kant's
  progress claim, which deflects both the epistemic problem (we cannot observe moral
  progress empirically) and the metaphysical problem (nature as teleological agent
  conflicts with critical philosophy). This reading is the main Kantian response to
  contemporary pessimism about progress and to postcolonial critiques that target
  substantive teleological content.

  POSITION: Constructivist-practical defense of Kantian progressivism; regulative reading.
  },
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  keywords = {Kant, philosophy-of-history, progress, perpetual-peace, High},
  abstract = {This article analyses the teleological argument justifying historical progress in Kant's Guarantee of Perpetual Peace. It starts by examining the controversies produced by Kant's claim that the teleology of nature supports the idea of a providential development of humanity towards moral progress and the possibility of achieving a cosmopolitan political constitution. It further illustrates how Kant's teleological argument in Perpetual Peace needs to be assessed with reference to two systematically relevant issues: first, the problem of coordination linked to the necessity of realizing the ‘highest good’ as a historical end of practical reason, and secondly the problem of continuity posed by the temporal limitation of all individual efforts to cultivate moral dispositions. To illustrate the implications of both issues for the teleological argument in Perpetual Peace , the article draws attention to some important developments in Kant's analysis of teleology following the Critique of Judgment .},
  sep_context = {Cited in SEP 'progress' entry alongside Kleingeld (1999): "While the idea that nature has a progressive plan sounds teleological, it is best read as a 'heuristic assumption' or a 'regulative principle' (Kleingeld 1999; Wood 1991; Moller 2021; Ypi 2010). That is, we should believe as if progress were nature's plan so that hope and responsible human action are possible."},
}

@article{ypi2024moralOught,
  author = {Ypi, Lea},
  title = {The moral ought in conjectural history},
  journal = {Critical Review of International Social and Political Philosophy},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {27},
  number = {6},
  pages = {991--1010},
  doi = {10.1080/13698230.2024.2344386},
  abstract = {ABSTRACT This article defends the importance of the idea of historical progress for constructivist justifications of moral normativity inspired by Kant's analysis of practical reason. Focusing on some key methodological requirements that must be satisfied for the constructivist vindication of practical normativity to succeed, the article focuses on the concept of purposiveness as it develops within Kant's moral and political philosophy. It concludes that without a critical notion of 'purposiveness' and related philosophical analysis of history, the constructivist rejection of scepticism is at risk of circularity.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Ypi argues that Kantian constructivism about moral normativity requires
  a concept of purposiveness — and hence an embedded philosophy of history — to avoid
  circularity in its rejection of scepticism. Without the idea of historical progress
  anchoring the point of moral reasoning, constructivist justification of practical
  normativity collapses into the very foundationalism it aims to escape.

  RELEVANCE: Extends and deepens the regulative reading of Kant's progress claim into
  the meta-ethical domain, showing that the philosophy of history is not an optional
  appendage to Kant's practical philosophy but is structurally required by it.
  Directly addresses the question of what would count as evidence for or against moral
  progress by arguing it must be understood within the framework of practical reason,
  not empirical observation.

  POSITION: Constructivist defense of the indispensability of historical progress for
  Kantian ethics.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, constructivism, progress, normativity, High}
}

@article{kleingeld2001nature,
  author = {Kleingeld, Pauline},
  title = {Nature or Providence? On the Theoretical and Moral Importance of Kant's Philosophy of History},
  journal = {American Catholic Philosophical Quarterly},
  year = {2001},
  volume = {75},
  number = {2},
  pages = {201--219},
  doi = {10.5840/acpq200175216},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kleingeld examines Kant's apparently oscillating use of "Nature" and
  "Providence" in his historical essays and argues that these terms function not as
  metaphysical agents but as rhetorical devices for the same regulative idea: that we
  may think of history as if guided toward human freedom. She shows this reading is
  morally indispensable — without it, the demand for moral effort in politics becomes
  motivationally groundless — while remaining theoretically neutral between theism and
  naturalism.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a canonical text for the "regulative" reading that defends Kant's
  philosophy of history against the charge of covert dogmatic metaphysics. The paper
  directly addresses why moral progress is practically indispensable even if theoretically
  unjustifiable, which is central to assessing the philosophical standing of the
  Geschichtszeichen argument in the Streit der Fakultaeten.

  POSITION: Regulative/moral defense of Kant's philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, philosophy-of-history, progress, providence, High},
  abstract = {Soulevant la question de la relation entre nature et providence dans la philosophie de l'histoire kantienne, l'A. montre que la preference de Kant pour le terme de providence dans «L'idee d'une histoire universelle» correspond au passage de la question theorique de l'ordre dans l'histoire a ne approche morale de l'histoire fondee sur le principe teleologique.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{williams2024progress,
  author = {Williams, Avery A.},
  title = {Is Progress Possible? Moral Improvement in Kant's Renewed Attempt},
  journal = {History of Political Thought},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {45},
  number = {2},
  pages = {327--346},
  doi = {10.53765/20512988.45.2.327},
  abstract = {This article seeks to understand the possibility of moral improvement in human social history through a close interpretation of Kant's A Renewed Attempt to Answer the Question: Is the Human Race Continually Improving? The article examines the ways in which Kant's arguments in this final piece, while often overlooked, complicate his philosophy of history as put forward in earlier works. Through this comparison, one sees important complications in Kant's thoughts on the philosophy of history at the end of his career, conceding that rightful progress towards a global republicanism is the most we can be sure of. This concession is striking considering Kant's ambitious goal at the outset to demonstrate the theoretical certainty of moral progress, going well beyond the practical optimism he advocates in earlier works. Yet, despite this concession, Kant is still optimistic about the possibility of moral progress at the end of the piece. This article suggests that Kant's Renewed Attempt is best understood as a window into Kant's grappling with the difficulties of constructing a philosophy of history that is consistent with his moral philosophy.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Williams argues that Kant's "Renewed Attempt to Answer the Question:
  Is the Human Race Continually Improving?" (the Contest of Faculties text) represents
  a significant late-career revision in which Kant retreats from ambitious claims about
  theoretical certainty of moral progress and limits himself to claiming rightful-political
  progress toward global republicanism. This concession reveals the difficulty of
  maintaining consistency between Kant's moral philosophy and his philosophy of history.

  RELEVANCE: The paper focuses precisely on the Geschichtszeichen argument and its
  epistemic credentials — the text in which Kant claims the French Revolution's
  enthusiastic reception functions as a "sign of history" for moral progress. Williams
  shows the limitations of this claim from within Kant's own system, making this the
  key contemporary analysis of the philosophical standing of Kant's most famous
  sign-of-history argument.

  POSITION: Internal Kantian critique of the ambitions of the progress claim; moderate
  reformist reading.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, Contest-of-Faculties, moral-progress, signs-of-history, High}
}

@article{marwah2022white,
  author = {Marwah, Inder},
  title = {White Progress: Kant, Race and Teleology},
  journal = {Kantian Review},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {27},
  number = {4},
  pages = {615--634},
  doi = {10.1017/S1369415422000334},
  abstract = {Abstract This article examines how Kant's conceptualizations of natural history and teleological judgement shape his understanding of human difference and race. I argue that the teleological framework encasing Kant's racial theory implies constraints on the capacity of non-whites to make moral progress. While commentators tend to approach Kant's racial theory in relation to his political theory, his late-life cosmopolitanism, and his treatments (or non-treatments) of colonialism, empire and slavery, the problem I focus on here is that race is itself only intelligible in relation to a teleological natural history limiting certain races' capacities to engage in humanity's moral vocation.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Marwah argues that Kant's racial theory is not an extraneous
  appendage to his critical philosophy but is structurally connected to his teleological
  framework: the same teleological judgment that identifies the vocation of humanity
  with rational moral development also assigns different races different capacities for
  that development, thereby limiting moral progress to whites. This is not merely an
  external inconsistency but an internal feature of Kant's teleology of history.

  RELEVANCE: Poses the sharpest internal challenge to the claim that Kantian
  progressivism can be salvaged by severing its racial ideology from its critical-moral
  core. If race and teleology are conceptually interconnected, the regulative reading
  of progress cannot simply excise racial hierarchy. Central to assessing the viability
  of Kantian resources for contemporary political philosophy.

  POSITION: Postcolonial/racial critique of Kantian teleology and progress narrative.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, race, teleology, progress, postcolonial-critique, High}
}

@book{allen2016end,
  author = {Allen, Amy},
  title = {The End of Progress},
  publisher = {Columbia University Press},
  year = {2016},
  doi = {10.7312/alle17324},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Allen argues that the normative foundations of critical theory —
  inherited from Kant and Hegel — are structured by a philosophy of history that
  takes European modernity as the telos of development, thereby replicating the
  colonial hierarchies they purport to criticize. Drawing on Foucault and Adorno,
  she proposes a decolonized critical theory that abandons progress as its normative
  anchor and replaces it with a critical problematization of sedimented historical
  standpoints (what she calls "unlearning").

  RELEVANCE: The book is the most systematic philosophical attempt to decolonize
  the Frankfurt School tradition and its Kantian inheritance. It challenges not just
  Kant's progress narrative but the progressive structure of practical reason itself,
  arguing that even the regulative/practical reading of progress carries ideological
  freight. Central to assessing postcolonial critiques of Kantian philosophy of history
  and the resources available for responding to them.

  POSITION: Postcolonial critical theory; against normative progressivism.
  },
  keywords = {progress, postcolonial, Frankfurt-School, critical-theory, Kant, High},
  abstract = {Allen argues that Frankfurt School critical theory (Habermas, Honneth, Forst) is structured by a conception of historical progress that implicitly takes European modernity as the telos of development, thereby replicating colonial hierarchies. Drawing on Adorno and Foucault, she proposes a decolonized critical theory that grounds normativity not in backward-looking accomplished progress but in a critical problematization of historically sedimented standpoints, which she calls unlearning.},
  abstract_source = {ndpr},
  sep_context = {Cited in 'progress' entry: "Kant is among the few exceptions during the Enlightenment to articulate a non-teleological conception of progress, although this reading is contentious (see Allen 2016; Gray 2004; Koselleck 2002)." Cited again in discussion of postcolonial critiques: "Drawing inspiration from Adorno and Michel Foucault, Amy Allen (2016) sees promise not so much in the power of reasoning or learning; reason, while not necessarily a form a madness, is always entangled with power. Progress begins therefore not from learning how to utilize the capacity to reason but in unlearning."},
}
@article{cook2020adorno,
  author = {Cook, Deborah},
  title = {Adorno, Kant and Enlightenment},
  journal = {Kantian Review},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {25},
  number = {4},
  pages = {541--557},
  doi = {10.1017/S1369415420000400},
  abstract = {Abstract Theodor W. Adorno often made reference to Immanuel Kant's famous essay on enlightenment. Although he denied that immaturity is self-incurred, the first section of this article will show that he adopted many of Kant's ideas about maturity in his philosophically informed critique of monopoly conditions under late capitalism. The second section will explore Adorno's claim that the educational system could foster maturity by encouraging critical reflection on the social conditions that have made us what we are. Finally, this article will demonstrate that Adorno links enlightenment to Kant's idea of a realm of ends.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Cook demonstrates that Adorno's relationship to Kant's Enlightenment
  essay is more complex than the Dialectic of Enlightenment's apparent rejection
  suggests: Adorno appropriates Kant's notion of intellectual maturity (Muendigkeit)
  while rejecting the thesis that immaturity is self-incurred, and he connects
  enlightenment not to instrumental reason's triumph but to the Kantian ideal of a
  realm of ends — showing how Adorno's critique is an internal critique from Kantian
  premises rather than an external rejection.

  RELEVANCE: Helps clarify exactly what in Kant's philosophy the Frankfurt School
  critique targets and what it preserves, which is essential for assessing whether
  Kantian resources can respond to Adorno/Horkheimer's challenge to Enlightenment
  progressivism. The connection between enlightenment and the realm of ends is
  directly relevant to Kant's philosophy of history as a history of moral communities.

  POSITION: Reconstructive engagement between Adorno and Kant; nuances the Frankfurt
  School critique.
  },
  keywords = {Adorno, Kant, Enlightenment, Frankfurt-School, maturity, Medium}
}

@article{iago2020unfettered,
  author = {Iago, Garmon D.},
  title = {Towards an Unfettered Critique: Adorno's Appropriations and Transformations of Kant's Enlightenment},
  journal = {Kantian Review},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {25},
  number = {4},
  pages = {631--654},
  doi = {10.1017/S1369415420000369},
  abstract = {Abstract Many recent commentators have noticed how Adorno, in his late works, borrows Kant's definition of enlightenment to define key areas of his own critical practice. These discussions, however, have failed to notice how these late borrowings present an image of Kant's enlightenment which is diametrically opposed to his previous discussions. By tracing the development of Adorno's engagement with Kant's essay, I discover Adorno deliberately sublating Kant's definition as to enable its incorporation into his own works. Further, the article will examine some problems which appear to arise for Adorno when borrowing Kant's definition of enlightenment in his late works, which coalesce around the topics of negativism and the prospects for societal change.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Iago traces the evolution of Adorno's engagement with Kant's "What
  is Enlightenment?" essay across his career, showing that Adorno's late appropriation
  of Kant's definition represents a deliberate sublation — preserving the emancipatory
  demand while negating its optimistic implication that immaturity is merely
  self-incurred. The article identifies genuine tensions in Adorno's position around
  negativism and the possibility of social change, which point to limits of the
  Frankfurt School critique.

  RELEVANCE: Provides detailed analysis of the Kant-Adorno relationship at the level
  of the Enlightenment essay rather than the Critique, enabling more precise assessment
  of what Adorno's critique of Enlightenment means for Kant's philosophy of history.
  The tension between negativism and the possibility of change maps directly onto
  debates about whether Kantian progressivism can be recovered after the dialectic
  of Enlightenment's critique.

  POSITION: Reconstruction and internal critique of Adorno's Kant reception.
  },
  keywords = {Adorno, Kant, Enlightenment, Frankfurt-School, negativism, Medium}
}

@article{james2018kantToSade,
  author = {James, David},
  title = {From Kant to Sade: a fragment of the history of philosophy in the Dialectic of Enlightenment},
  journal = {British Journal for the History of Philosophy},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {26},
  number = {3},
  pages = {557--577},
  doi = {10.1080/09608788.2018.1437537},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: James analyzes the "Juliette" excursus in the Dialectic of
  Enlightenment — Adorno and Horkheimer's claim that the philosophical passage
  "from Kant to Sade" is necessary, not contingent, because Kantian moral formalism
  (the categorical imperative as formal law indifferent to content) can be turned to
  destructive ends as easily as to enlightened ones. James reconstructs this argument
  and evaluates its success as a reading of Kant, arguing that it misidentifies
  Kantian morality's relationship to sensuous nature.

  RELEVANCE: The "from Kant to Sade" excursus is the Dialectic's most direct
  engagement with Kant's practical philosophy and its relevance for the progress
  thesis. James's article provides the clearest philosophical reconstruction of this
  argument, enabling precise assessment of whether Adorno/Horkheimer's reading of
  Kant is accurate and whether their critique of moral formalism as potentially
  producing barbarism undermines Kant's philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Critical examination of the Adorno/Horkheimer argument about Kant and
  moral formalism.
  },
  keywords = {Adorno, Horkheimer, Kant, Sade, moral-formalism, Medium},
  abstract = {In this paper, I set out (1) to consider the extent to which Horkheimer and Adorno's account of the transition from Kant's philosophy to key features of the novels of the Marquis de Sade in the Second Excursus of their Dialectic of Enlightenment can be viewed as a fragment of the ‘history of philosophy’ and (2) to explain this account in a way that allows us to ask whether it succeeds in establishing a necessary connection between Kant's philosophy and Sade's novels. In connection with (2), a particular problem emerges. This problem concerns the role played by a non-instrumental form of reason in Horkheimer and Adorno's attempt to establish an essential connection between Kant's theoretical philosophy and Sade's novels, in which the practical implications of the theoretical employment of reason allegedly become explicit. It will be shown that, despite appearances to the contrary, an employment of reason of the relevant type is not identified by Kant in the Critique of Pure Reason.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{golob2021revolution,
  author = {Golob, Sacha},
  title = {Kant on revolution as a sign of moral progress},
  journal = {Journal of Philosophy of Education},
  year = {2021},
  volume = {55},
  number = {6},
  pages = {977--989},
  doi = {10.1111/1467-9752.12598},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Golob examines Kant's account of the French Revolution as a
  Geschichtszeichen — a sign or symptom of an underlying moral predisposition in
  humanity — arguing that the epistemic structure of this claim is more sophisticated
  than critics allow. The enthusiasm of spectators (not participants) functions as a
  diagnostic symptom analogous to aesthetic judgment: it reveals a moral predisposition
  without grounding a predictive claim about future history.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most targeted contemporary analysis of the Geschichtszeichen
  argument in the Contest of Faculties, which is the philosophical centerpiece of
  Domain 7. Golob's reading of the epistemic structure of the sign argument is
  essential for assessing whether Kant's claim about the French Revolution constitutes
  a defensible form of historical knowledge or a philosophically unjustifiable leap.

  POSITION: Sympathetic reconstruction of the sign-of-history argument as
  symptomatological rather than predictive.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, French-Revolution, signs-of-history, moral-progress, Contest-of-Faculties, High},
  abstract = {This paper addresses a central aspect of Kant's theory of moral progress and its links to both political violence and pedagogy. Kant claims in the Conflict of the Faculties that the reaction to the French Revolution demonstrates that the ‘human race has always progressed and will further progress toward the better’. It thus constitutes a ‘historical sign’, justifying belief in moral development. This paper argues for three points. First, I contend that these claims have been widely misunderstood: in particular, most commentators are guilty of projecting Hegelian arguments onto Kant. Second, I advance a new reading of Kant's account of the ‘historical sign’ focusing on the epistemic status of the spectator and the role of ‘enthusiasm’. One consequence is that the case for progress is fundamentally different from that in Kant's other writings. Third, I show how these results inform Kant's stance on education and the possibility of what he calls ‘popular enlightenment’. Ironically, the details of Kant's argument may seem to support pessimism rather than any celebration of human progress: the appeal to education is crucial in making that final leap.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{moran2012community,
  author = {Moran, Kate A.},
  title = {Community and Progress in Kant's Moral Philosophy},
  publisher = {Catholic University of America Press},
  year = {2012},
  doi = {10.2307/j.ctt284v4f},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Moran argues that Kant's concept of moral progress is grounded not
  in individual moral development but in the project of building ethical community —
  the Ethical Commonwealth described in the Religion — which provides the social and
  institutional conditions for progress that individual moral agency alone cannot
  secure. Progress is possible, on this reading, precisely because morality is
  inherently communal in its highest expression.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most sustained book-length defense of the coherence of
  Kant's moral progress concept within the framework of the critical philosophy.
  By locating progress in the ethical community rather than in individual moral
  trajectories, Moran's account addresses the internal consistency challenge: it
  explains how moral progress can be a collective phenomenon without violating the
  noumenal character of individual moral agency.

  POSITION: Kantian defense of collective moral progress via ethical community.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, moral-progress, ethical-community, highest-good, Medium},
  abstract = {Immanuel Kant's moral philosophy has often been criticised for ignoring a crucial dimension of community in its account of the lives that agents ought to lead. Historical and contemporary critics alike often paint Kant's moral theory, with its emphasis on rationality, as overly formalistic and unrealistically isolating. Against these criticisms, Kate A. Moran argues that Kant's moral philosophy reserves a central role for community in several important respects. In the first part of her book, Moran asserts that Kant's most developed account of the goal toward which agents ought to strive is actually a kind of ethical community. Indeed, Kant claims that agents have a duty to pursue this goal. Moran argues that this duty entails a concern for the development of agents' moral characters and capacities for moral reasoning, as well as the institutions and relationships that aid in this development. Next, Moran examines three specific social institutions and relationships that, according to Kant, help develop moral character and moral reasoning. In three separate chapters, Moran examines the role that moral education, friendship, and participation in civil society play in developing agents' moral capacities. Far from being mere afterthoughts in Kant's moral system, Moran maintains that these institutions are crucial in bringing about the end of an ethical community. The text draws on a wide range of Immanuel Kant's writings, including his texts on moral and political philosophy and his lectures on ethics, pedagogy, and anthropology. Though the book is grounded in an analysis of Kant's writing, it also puts forward the novel claim that Kant's theory is centrally concerned with the relationships we have in our day-to-day lives. It will, therefore, be an invaluable tool in understanding both the complexities ofKant's moral philosophy, and how even a liberal, deontological theory like Kant's can give a satisfying account of the importance of community in our moral lives.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{stolzenberg2025enthusiasm,
  author = {Stolzenberg, Juergen},
  title = {Kant on Enthusiasm},
  journal = {Kantian journal},
  year = {2025},
  volume = {44},
  number = {1},
  pages = {40--67},
  doi = {10.5922/0207-6918-2025-1-2},
  abstract = {Kant's theory of enthusiasm has received relatively little attention in Kant studies. This is surprising in view of the fact that Kant was preoccupied with the theme of enthusiasm throughout his life. One of the reasons may be that for Kant enthusiasm is an affect. Therefore, it cannot be used to justify ethics. On closer examination, however, a more differentiated picture emerges. In addition to pathological enthusiasm, Kant recognises an aesthetically sublime enthusiasm, and in his reflections on the reaction to the events of the French Revolution, Kant coined the concept of a true enthusiasm, related to the ideas of freedom and justice. Finally, Kant introduces the concept of the enthusiasm of good resolution. It is a preliminary stage of the feeling of respect for the moral law in moral education. It is no longer regarded as an affect, but is under the guidance of reason. The first part discusses the partly incompatible conceptions of enthusiasm. The second part examines Jean-Francois Lyotard's interpretation and critique of Kant's philosophical-historical theory of enthusiasm. In the face of the devastating catastrophes of the twentieth century, Lyotard sees no reason for the enthusiasm that Kant perceived in the people's reaction to the French Revolution and interpreted as a prognostic sign of future history. It has been replaced by a general world-weariness. However, Lyotard's critique of Kant is based on a historicist fallacy. The reference to historical catastrophes is not an argument against the a priori validity of the ideas of freedom and justice and against the endeavour for their realisation.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Stolzenberg distinguishes three types of Kantian enthusiasm —
  pathological, aesthetically sublime, and "true" enthusiasm tied to ideas of freedom
  and justice — and uses this typology to respond to Lyotard's argument that the
  catastrophes of the twentieth century (particularly the Holocaust) have rendered
  Kant's appeal to revolutionary enthusiasm as a historical sign morally and
  epistemically untenable. Stolzenberg argues that Lyotard commits a historicist
  fallacy by treating empirical catastrophe as evidence against the a priori validity
  of moral ideas.

  RELEVANCE: Directly engages the most powerful contemporary challenge to Kant's
  Geschichtszeichen argument: that the twentieth century's atrocities retroactively
  undermine the epistemic standing of revolutionary enthusiasm as a sign of moral
  progress. The response via the a priori status of moral ideas also engages the
  question of what evidence could count against moral progress claims.

  POSITION: Kantian defense of the historical sign argument against Lyotard's
  post-Holocaust pessimism.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, enthusiasm, French-Revolution, Lyotard, signs-of-history, Medium}
}

@incollection{merritt2023mendelssohn,
  author = {Merritt, Melissa},
  title = {Mendelssohn and Kant on Human Progress},
  booktitle = {Kant on Freedom and Human Nature},
  publisher = {Routledge},
  year = {2023},
  pages = {190--208},
  doi = {10.4324/9781003259985-15},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Merritt examines the debate between Mendelssohn and Kant about
  whether moral progress is possible for humankind as a species or only for
  individuals. Mendelssohn maintained that humanity "fluctuates within fixed limits"
  (a position Kant labels "abderitism"), arguing that progress at the species level
  is incoherent because virtue attaches only to individual wills. Merritt argues
  against Paul Guyer's claim that Kant's own freedom commitments trap him in
  abderitism, showing that the Stoic background of both thinkers illuminates why
  Kant can coherently posit species-level progress without committing to individual
  moral development in history.

  RELEVANCE: The Mendelssohn-Kant dispute is the historical debate that defines the
  problem of moral progress for Kant's philosophy of history: if moral improvement
  only tracks individual moral trajectories, collective historical progress is
  philosophically unjustifiable. Merritt's chapter provides the most recent careful
  analysis of this debate and its implications for the coherence of Kant's philosophy
  of history.

  POSITION: Defends coherence of species-level moral progress against Mendelssohnian
  individualist objection.
  },
  keywords = {Kant, Mendelssohn, moral-progress, species, abderitism, Medium},
  abstract = {The chapter replies to Paul Guyer's (2020) account of the debate between Mendelssohn and Kant about whether humankind makes continual moral progress. Mendelssohn maintained that progress can only be the remit of individuals, and that humankind only “continually fluctuates within fixed limits”. Kant dubs Mendelssohn's position “abderitism” and explicitly rejects it. But Guyer contends that Kant's own theory of freedom commits him, malgré lui, to abderitism. Guyer's risky interpretive position is not supported by examination of the relevant texts in their intellectual context. This chapter identifies the historical origins of the term abderitism, which here signifies the independence of individual progress from social conditions. By contrast, Kant argues that individual progress cannot be independent of the progress of the species, acting as a corporate agent. This arresting position, the chapter shows, must be understood in light of the Stoic ethical-teleological presuppositions generally accepted in eighteenth-century German discussion of human progress.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}
